    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a>'s rise rested on Ming protection: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-he">Zheng He</a> called there in 1407, and in exchange for regular tribute the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a> shielded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parameswara">Parameswara</a> from the threat of Siamese attack.</li><li>Under Mansur Shah (r. 1459–1477) <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> conquered Pahang, received Indragiri, Jambi, Tungkal and Siantan from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> and vassalised Siak, ruling much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a> and the east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> became the centre of Islamic learning in the archipelago; Classical Malay became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a> of Maritime <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> and Jawi script the medium of its religious and intellectual exchange.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Afonso de Albuquerque</a> said that taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> from the Moors would ruin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> no <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> but what her merchants bought in Portugal; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tome-pires">Tomé Pires</a> wrote that &quot;whoever is lord of Malacca shall have his hands on the throat of Venice&quot;.</p></aside>
    </section>

