 x2="402.7" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="402.7" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="402.7" y="164" text-anchor="middle">5</text><title>1459–1477: Mansur Shah conquers Pahang with a fleet of 200 ships and gains Indragiri, Jambi, Tungkal and Siantan from Majapahit; he dies at the peak of Malacca's splendour.</title></g><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="727.0" x2="727.0" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="727.0" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="727.0" y="164" text-anchor="middle">6</text><title>1509: On 1 August Diogo Lopes de Sequeira arrives from Portugal; a plot to kill him fails, but several of his men are taken captive.</title></g><g class="rf-ev"><line x1="740.0" x2="740.0" y1="160" y2="166"/><circle cx="740.0" cy="160" r="8"/><text x="740.0" y="164" text-anchor="middle">7</text><title>1511: On 15 August Afonso de Albuquerque captures Malacca after a failed assault on 25 July; Mahmud Shah flees south.</title></g><text class="rf-axis" x="20" y="200">1400 CE</text><text class="rf-axis" x="740" y="200" text-anchor="end">1511 CE</text></svg><figcaption>Relative power on the Institute's 0–10 scale (each profile scaled to its own peak). Numbers mark the key events below.</figcaption></figure><ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1400</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parameswara">Parameswara</a>, fleeing the sack of Singapura, founds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> at a fishing village of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orang-laut">Orang Laut</a> at the mouth of the Bertam River.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1405</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a> sends Yin Qing to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-he">Zheng He</a> follows in 1407 and takes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parameswara">Parameswara</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, recognising him as its ruler.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1424–1444</span><span class="rf-text">Raja Tengah takes the name Muhammad Shah and the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>, Islamises the administration and issues the Maritime Laws of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a>.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1446</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> invades by land and is beaten with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tun-perak">Tun Perak</a>, who becomes Bendahara; a Siamese naval attack is repelled at Batu Pahat in 1456.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1459–1477</span><span class="rf-text">Mansur Shah conquers Pahang with a fleet of 200 ships and gains Indragiri, Jambi, Tungkal and Siantan from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a>; he dies at the peak of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a>'s splendour.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1509</span><span class="rf-text">On 1 August Diogo Lopes de Sequeira arrives from Portugal; a plot to kill him fails, but several of his men are taken captive.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1511</span><span class="rf-text">On 15 August <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Afonso de Albuquerque</a> captures <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> after a failed assault on 25 July; Mahmud Shah flees south.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1526</span><span class="rf-text">The Portuguese raze Mahmud Shah's base at Bintan; he dies at Kampar two years later, and his sons found the Sultanates of Perak and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a>.</span></li></ol>
