    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/parameswara"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parameswara.jpg" alt="Parameswara" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/parameswara">Parameswara</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Retrato_de_Parameswara.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">L joo., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a> had been dominated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a>, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> weakened it in the 11th century, and by the end of the 13th by the Javanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singhasari">Singhasari</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a>. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-annals">Malay Annals</a>, a prince of Palembang, Seri Teri Buana, who claimed descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, landed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temasek">Temasek</a> in 1299 and was made king of Singapura by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orang-laut">Orang Laut</a>, the sea people once loyal to Srivijaya. Singapura grew rich in the 14th century, alarming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> to the north and Majapahit to the south, and after at least two invasions it was sacked by one of them in 1398. Its last king fled to the west coast of the peninsula: Portuguese sources call him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parameswara">Parameswara</a>, a Palembang usurper of Singapura's throne; Malay sources call him Iskandar Shah, fifth king of Singapura and descendant of Seri Teri Buana.</p><p>He reached a fishing village of the Orang Laut at the mouth of the Bertam River, spared by the Majapahit fleets that had sacked Singapura, Langkasuka and Pasai and since the 1370s a refuge that already held Buddhists from the north, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> from Palembang and Muslims from Pasai. Legend says Parameswara, resting under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> tree, watched a mouse deer outwit his hunting dog and declared "this place is excellent, even the mouse deer is formidable; it is best that we establish a kingdom here". The founding is conventionally dated about 1400, though earlier dates have been proposed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/spice-trade.jpg" alt="Spice trade" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">Spice trade</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spices_in_an_Indian_market.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">judepics, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> grew from a settlement into an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/entrepot">entrepôt</a> within a century, thanks to its position on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a> and the demand for goods from East and West. Ships from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryukyu-islands">Ryukyu Islands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Maluku Islands</a> came in on the northeast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> from December to January, and ships for the Indian coasts, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> left on the southwest monsoon. Kedah, Jambi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a> were rivals, but none matched Malacca's security, praised in Chinese records of the mid-15th century, nor its port: warehouses where merchants stored goods while waiting for the winds, elephants to carry cargo, and four shahbandars who administered the traders by region. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orang-laut">Orang Laut</a> patrolled the seas against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> and guided ships in; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, Indians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> settled, and the population early rose to 2,000.</p><p>Malacca produced little of its own: small amounts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> and gold and dried fish, with even the salt for curing the fish brought from elsewhere, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, vegetables, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and fish supplied by its trading partners. Its function was collection and redistribution: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cloves">cloves</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nutmeg">nutmeg</a> and mace from the Maluku Islands; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> textiles from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coromandel-coast">Coromandel</a> and Malabar coasts and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and iron from China; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camphor">camphor</a>, sandalwood, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>, fish roe and seaweed from the archipelago. Tin ingots cast in the shape of a peck, each just over a pound, served as a currency unique to Malacca, alongside gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor.jpg" alt="Yongle Emperor" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_assis_de_l%27empereur_Ming_Chengzu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Megat Iskandar Shah (r. 1414–1424) walled the city with four guarded gates and built a fenced fortress at its centre for the treasury and stores. The chief enemy was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>, whose ambitions drove the king to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1418 to seek protection, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a> warned the Siamese ruler in October 1419. In 1446 Ayutthaya invaded by land and was beaten with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tun-perak">Tun Perak</a>, chief of Klang, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Muzaffar Shah made Bendahara; in 1456, under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borommatrailokkanat">Trailokanat</a>, the Siamese came by sea, and Tun Perak and Tun Hamzah, met them off Batu Pahat, where the more advanced Malaccan navy drove them off and chased them to Singapura.</p><p>Mansur Shah's fleet of two hundred ships under Tun Perak and nineteen hulubalangs crushed Pahang and carried off its ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> Dewa Sura and his daughter; Pahang, Kampar and Indragiri later rebelled and were subdued, and Malaccan forces restored Sultan Zainal Abidin of Pasai against the relatives who had toppled him. After the Vietnamese castrated and enslaved its envoys in 1469 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> refrained from retaliation and complained to the Ming court instead. The Laksamana, greatest of them Hang Tuah, commanded the navy and policed the strait. Portugal was another kind of enemy: Diogo Lopes de Sequeira's men were seized in 1509, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Afonso de Albuquerque</a>'s armada, arriving in April 1511 to sever the Islamic and Venetian spice trade, failed on 25 July and took the city on 15 August 1511.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tome-pires"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tome-pires.jpg" alt="Tomé Pires" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tome-pires">Tomé Pires</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tom%C3%A9_Pires_stamp.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At the head of the sultanate stood the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>, an absolute monarch whose right to rule rested on legitimate lineage in the Srivijayan tradition, renamed with the coming of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> as daulat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. The legal codes named four chief officials. The Bendahara, like a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>, advised the Sultan and kept relations with foreign states, the highest office open to a commoner; the fifth Bendahara, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tun-perak">Tun Perak</a>, excelled in war and diplomacy. Below him came the Penghulu bendahari, the treasurer; the Temenggung, chief of police and security; and the Laksamana, head of the navy and chief emissary, who enforced the Undang-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/undang">Undang</a> Laut Melaka. Four Shahbandars served the trading communities of the port by region, and a Mandulika governed conquered territories.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> was governed by written law, the Undang-Undang Melaka, also called the Hukum <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanun">Kanun</a> Melaka, and the Maritime Laws, shaped by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> tradition, Islam and indigenous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adat">adat</a>. Raja Tengah, hearing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">ulama</a> Saiyid Abdul Aziz, took the name Muhammad Shah and the title Sultan and made customs, protocol, bureaucracy and commerce conform to Islam; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tome-pires">Tomé Pires</a>, however, says Megat Iskandar Shah converted at 72, and it is agreed that Islam was firmly established under Muzaffar Shah (r. 1445–1459). Succession could be violent: Abu Syahid Shah (r. 1444–1446), controlled by his mother's cousin Raja Rokan, was killed with him in a court plot that installed Raja Kasim as Muzaffar Shah.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/wali-songo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wali-songo.jpg" alt="Wali Songo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/wali-songo">Wali Songo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Borobdur9205.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Greenland4, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> heralded the golden age of the Malay sultanates. It was the first Malay Muslim state to become a regional maritime power and the first centralised polity to rule the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a>, and for that it is called the spiritual birthplace of Malaysia. From its study centres in Upeh, on the north bank of the river, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> spread to Jambi, Kampar, Bengkalis, Siak, Aru and the Karimun Islands, through the peninsula, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>; the rulers of Kampar and Indragiri converted through Muzaffar Shah's influence, and two of Java's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wali-songo">Wali Sanga</a>, Sunan Bonang and Sunan Kalijaga, are said to have studied in Malacca.</p><p>In Malacca Classical Malay became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a> of Maritime <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> and Jawi script the medium of religious and intellectual exchange; its traditions, laws, dress, cuisine, performing and martial arts and court rituals set the standard of Malay culture and the pattern for later sultanates, a Malayisation that spread through the region. Rulers came to Malacca after their coronation for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>'s blessing, and deposed rulers from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Moluccas</a>, Rokan and Terengganu sought his aid. Malacca carried on the struggle of Singapura and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> against Java: by the mid-15th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> could no longer control it, and the spread of Islam into the Javanese interior hastened Majapahit's fall in the early 16th century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/a-famosa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/a-famosa.jpg" alt="A Famosa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/a-famosa">A Famosa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Famosa_Fortress.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chongkian, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> was at its height about 1500, when Mahmud Shah rejected the overlordship of both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> and the declining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a>, but dissension between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> and his Bendahara Tun Mutahir weakened the court. When Diogo Lopes de Sequeira arrived on 1 August 1509 with a letter from Manuel I, the Tamil Muslims close to Tun Mutahir and the Gujarati merchants preached holy war against the infidels; a plot to kill de Sequeira and seize his fleet leaked, and he escaped leaving several men captive. In April 1511 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Afonso de Albuquerque</a> arrived to sever Malacca's Islamic and Venetian trade, failed on 25 July and took the city on 15 August 1511. The Portuguese built the fortress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/a-famosa">A Famosa</a> from the stones of Muslim graves and mosques.</p><p>Mahmud Shah fled, tried repeatedly to retake the city, was driven to Pahang and then to Bintan, from which his forces raided and blockaded the Portuguese until they razed Bintan in 1526; he died two years later at Kampar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>. His son Muzaffar Shah was invited to rule the north of the peninsula as Sultan of Perak, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-riayat-shah-ii">Alauddin Riayat Shah II</a> founded the Sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-malacca">Portuguese Malacca</a> never controlled the trade it had seized; short of money and men, it watched Asian traders bypass it for Johor and other ports, and the single policed emporium of the strait gave way to scattered rivals. The fort fell only once, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and Johor in January 1641.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Malacca Sultanate, founded about 1400 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parameswara">Parameswara</a> of Singapura under Ming protection, made its port the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/entrepot">entrepôt</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a> and the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and Malay culture in the archipelago, ruling the peninsula and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> under Mansur Shah before falling to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Albuquerque</a>'s Portuguese on 15 August 1511.</em></p>

